# Hemp leaf banner pattern — the loom's "Cannabis Leaf" (roadmap D1).
#
# ## PLACEHOLDER. Read this before spending time on it.
#
# Warlon Mhite asked on 2026-09-08 for real art rather than a stand-in, and this is the third
# attempt at it, but it is NOT finished and it is parked deliberately. In game the leaf reads as
# *two* motifs — a fan, then a separate funnel below it — even though the file is one continuous
# shape (dump the PNG's alpha and you can see it is contiguous from the fingers to the palm). So
# something about how the flag samples this file does not match the net layout described below,
# and until that is understood, drawing a better leaf is drawing in the dark.
#
# **The next step is a ruler, not another leaf**: write a bar every five rows across v 0..40, look
# at the banner, and read off which texture rows land where. `shot.py --reload` exists now
# precisely to make that loop cheap. A first attempt at this was made and lost to a client restart.
#

# ## The canvas is not the file
#
# A banner pattern is a 64x64 PNG, but the flag is a 20x40x1 box and the file is that box's
# net. Only these rectangles are ever seen:
#
#     front face   x  1..20, y 1..40
#     back face    x 22..41, y 1..40   MIRRORED: back_x = 42 - front_x
#     side strips  x 0 and x 21, y 1..40   (paint these only where the art touches an edge)
#     top strip    y 0, x 1..20
#
# Everything else is dead space. So the drawing is one 20x40 leaf written twice, the second
# time reversed. Measured out of the client jar: base.png's alpha bbox is 0,0..41,40, and
# half_vertical.png paints x0..11 on the front and x31..41 on the back, which is what proves
# both the mirror and the side-strip bleed. Edit both copies together or the banner reads
# differently from each side.
#
# The shield is a separate, smaller canvas: a 10x20 window at x 2..11, y 2..21, front only,
# and both files are required — vanilla ships a shield sprite for every pattern, and a
# missing one draws as the magenta missing texture on any shield somebody applies this to.
#
# ## Why it is one flat tone
#
# The renderer multiplies this sprite by the layer's dye colour, so a grey pixel is only a
# darker shade of whatever the player dyed it. Vanilla's own patterns are effectively flat
# masks (E3-F3, about a 6% spread, and that is cloth noise rather than shading). Internal
# shading here would read as dirt at twenty pixels wide and would fight all sixteen dyes.
# The linter's "only 1 colour" note is expected and correct for a mask.
#
# ## What makes it a cannabis leaf and not a maple leaf
#
# The gaps. Blades that stay narrow with open space between them, the lowest pair kept as
# short stubs rather than swelling into lobes, and the base fused into a solid triangle only
# in the last four rows. Fill the gaps in and it is a maple leaf; that is the single failure
# this shape is built to avoid. Seven full leaflets do not fit — at 20 px wide a row can hold
# the central blade plus one pair with a gap either side, no more — so the lower pair is
# staggered further down the stalk instead, which is how a real leaf is arranged anyway.

@palette default
. = none
w = F1F1F1        flat mask; the dye colour is multiplied over it at render time

@texture entity/banner/hemp_leaf 64x64 type=plant
